In 2026, From an ArXiv Pre-Print astronomers using the [[James Webb Space Telescope|JWST]] confirmed the presence of a strong He II emission from a region designated as ''Hebe'',<ref name=":Roberto Maiolino et al (2026)">{{cite arXiv |last1=Maiolino |first1=Roberto |title=The search for Population III: Confirmation of a HeII emitter with no metal lines at z=10.6 |date=2026-04-06 |eprint=2603.20362 |last2=Übler |first2=Hannah |last3=Perna |first3=Michele |last4=Witstok |first4=Joris |last5=Jones |first5=Gareth C. |last6=Perez-Gonzalez |first6=Pablo G. |last7=Nakajima |first7=Kimihiko |last8=Rusta |first8=Elka |last9=Salvadori |first9=Stefania |class=astro-ph.GA }}</ref> located approximately 3 kiloparsecs from GN-z11.<ref name=":Roberto Maiolino et al (2026)" /> The He II emission is spectrally resolved into two distinct components (C1 and C2), where C1 region is thought to potential contain [[Stellar population#Population III stars|Population III]] stars and C2 region to contain [[Stellar population#Population II stars|Population II]] stars.<ref name=":Roberto Maiolino et al (2026)" /> Population III stars scenario is taken into consideration due to absence of metal emission lines while other scenarios have also been taken into consideration like metal-poor [[Wolf-Rayet stars]] and [[Direct collapse black hole]] or [[Primordial black hole]], but Population III star scenario is thought to be the most likely.<ref name=":Roberto Maiolino et al (2026)" />

